What's already right — worth saying plainly, because a lot of portfolios get these wrong: verbatim separation of content into src/data/*, one concern per component, consistent Tailwind token usage, rel="noreferrer" on every target="_blank" link, real <label> elements, a lang attribute, scroll-mt on anchor targets, and a lockfile committed with no dist/ in git.
01 — Overall structure
The file organisation is the strongest part of this project
src/
├─ components/
│  ├─ layouts/   Layout · Sidebar · MobileNav
│  ├─ sections/  Hero · About · Projects · Skills · Contact
│  └─ ui/        ProjectCard · SectionHeading · SkillBadge · BrandIcons
├─ pages/        Home
├─ data/         projects.js · skills.js
├─ App.jsx  main.jsx  index.css
Three tiers — layouts (page chrome), sections (page regions) and ui (reusable primitives) — is exactly the right decomposition for a one-page site. Content lives in src/data/, so adding a project is a data edit, not a JSX edit. That's the decision that will save the most time later.

Minor structural notes
Dead files. src/App.css (a 4-line universal reset) is never imported, and the reset is already handled by Tailwind's Preflight. public/icons.svg (5.6 KB of unused symbols) is referenced nowhere.
Naming drift. The Hero id is hero but its nav label is "Overview"; the section id="projects" renders the heading "Featured Work & Projects". Not a bug — just two vocabularies for one thing.
Footer placement. The site footer is hard-coded inside pages/Home.jsx, so it would be duplicated on any second page. It belongs in Layout.jsx next to the sidebar.
No LICENSE. A public repo with no licence is legally "all rights reserved" — fine for a portfolio, but it stops anyone reading the code as a reference. MIT is one file.
02 — Code quality
Readable and consistent; the main debt is duplication between the two navs
The JSX is easy to follow: small components, descriptive prop names, no premature abstraction, and no useEffect where a derived value would do. There is exactly one useEffect in the whole codebase. Lint is clean (eslint . → exit 0, zero warnings).

