Medium
The scroll-spy re-implements IntersectionObserver by hand — and hardcodes the section list
Sidebar.jsx:23–43
The handler reads offsetTop and offsetHeight on every scroll event, forcing a layout read each time, and it repeats the section ids a third time inside the effect. I tested it in a real browser at every section — it works (correct nav item highlighted at #hero, #about, #projects, #skills and #contact, and also when loading /#skills directly) — so this is a maintainability and efficiency note, not a bug.

useEffect(() => {
  const observer = new IntersectionObserver(
    (entries) => {
      const visible = entries.filter((e) => e.isIntersecting);
      if (visible.length) setActiveSection(visible[0].target.id);
    },
    { rootMargin: "-45% 0px -45% 0px" }  // active = what crosses mid-viewport
  );
  navItems.forEach(({ id }) => {
    const el = document.getElementById(id);
    if (el) observer.observe(el);
  });
  return () => observer.disconnect();
}, []);
Why it matters: drops ~1 forced reflow per scroll tick, kills the magic 200 px offset, and removes the duplicated id list — the ids come from the same array the links are rendered from.

