03 — HTML / CSS / React best practices
Solid semantics and responsiveness; one layout bug at short viewports
Semantics are genuinely good: <main>, <aside>, <nav>, <article> and <header> are used for what they mean, heading order is a clean h1 → h2 → h3 with no skipped levels, and anchor targets carry scroll-mt-6. The mobile↔desktop split (sticky sidebar above md, top bar + drawer below) is the right pattern and the breakpoint is correct.

Medium
The desktop sidebar is taller than a short laptop viewport, and it cannot scroll
Sidebar.jsx:48
The aside is h-screen with justify-between and no overflow rule. At a 1280×720 window the content is 664 px tall inside a 577 px box — measured in the browser — so the footer block (the Foundry callout, the social icons and the copyright line) is pushed 87 px below the fold, and because overflow-y computes to visible it's clipped with no scrollbar to reach it. It's visible in the screenshot of the running build as a cut-off card at the bottom of the rail.

// Sidebar.jsx:48 — collide-safe: let the rail scroll instead of clipping
<aside className="w-72 ... h-screen sticky top-0 shrink-0 overflow-y-auto">
Why it matters: 720 pixel-tall laptop windows and any browser with a large bookmarks bar are the common case. Right now those visitors simply lose your GitHub/LinkedIn/email links. A tighter fix is justify-start gap-6 plus overflow-y-auto so the social block scrolls into reach rather than being pinned.

