05 — Performance
Fast in practice — but the bundle is heavier than this page needs
Measured from the real production build served locally:

Asset	Raw	Gzipped	Verdict
index.js	265.9 kB	80.6 kB	Heavy for one page
index.css	36.2 kB	6.6 kB	Fine
favicon.svg	9.6 kB	—	Large for an icon
Images / fonts	0	0	Excellent — no image payload at all
DOM interactive	97 ms (local)	Fast
The most impressive performance fact about this site is what isn't there: zero images, zero web fonts, zero third-party scripts. That's why it feels instant despite the JS weight.

Medium
The entire app ships as a single un-split 266 kB chunk
vite.config.js
One index.js contains React, ReactDOM and every icon and section. There's no manualChunks, and the React Compiler + Babel pipeline adds build weight without buying much on a page with two pieces of state. Most of the 266 kB is ReactDOM itself — unavoidable for React — so the realistic win is splitting vendor from app so the vendor chunk can be cached across deploys.

// vite.config.js — vendor chunk becomes cacheable across deploys
export default defineConfig({
  plugins: [tailwindcss(), react(), babel({ presets: [reactCompilerPreset()] })],
  build: {
    rollupOptions: {
      output: {
        manualChunks: {
          react: ["react", "react-dom", "react/jsx-runtime"],
          icons: ["lucide-react"],
        },
      },
    },
  },
});
Bigger question worth asking: a 5-section static page has almost no interactivity — two pieces of state (nav open, project filter) and one form. If you ever want a dramatically smaller site, this is a prime candidate for Astro or plain HTML. That's a rewrite, not a fix, so it's listed as optional below — not a recommendation to do it now.

Low
React Compiler is enabled but has essentially nothing to optimise
vite.config.js:5–9 · package.json
babel-plugin-react-compiler and @rolldown/plugin-babel run on every build. The codebase has one useEffect and two useStates — there is no re-render problem for it to solve. It costs build time and a Node ≥ 22.12 requirement (see dependency section) for no measurable runtime benefit here. Removing it simplifies the toolchain considerably.

